# Go Architecture Rules for Ultra-High-Performance Graph Traversal

You are building an in-memory Bidirectional BFS engine to find paths through the Wikipedia link network (7M+ nodes, 430M+ edges). 

Because this application handles a massive dataset, you must optimize strictly for CPU cache locality and zero Garbage Collector (GC) pointer-scanning overhead. Follow these structural constraints flawlessly.

## 1. Data Structure Layout (Zero Pointers, Pure Slices)
* **Rule:** Do NOT use structs with pointers, maps, or nested object references to represent the graph nodes or edges. 
* **Implementation:** Use a flat, array-backed adjacency list using contiguous primitive slices (`[]uint32`). The Go GC must view this memory as "dull" (containing no pointers to scan).
* **Graph Representation Template:**
  ```go
  type WikipediaGraph struct {
      // Index represents the Article ID. 
      // Value points to the starting index in the Edges slice.
      Offsets []uint32 
      // One giant contiguous allocation holding all destination IDs.
      Edges   []uint32 
  }
```
